WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:01.033 --> 00:00:02.267
-We're here with Chris Hayes.

00:00:02.267 --> 00:00:04.100
Second show after the end
of the writers' strike.

00:00:04.100 --> 00:00:05.667
We're seeing a real
interesting moment

00:00:05.667 --> 00:00:07.534
with labor organizing
in America.

00:00:07.534 --> 00:00:09.968
Obviously, SAG-AFTRA
is still on strike.

00:00:09.968 --> 00:00:14.267
We saw Biden on the picket line
with the autoworkers.

00:00:14.267 --> 00:00:16.834
You know, I think we all think
about strikes as, you know,

00:00:16.834 --> 00:00:19.133
wages, benefits,
working conditions,

00:00:19.133 --> 00:00:22.501
but what else about this moment
do you think is pushing

00:00:22.501 --> 00:00:24.601
what we see is a very
strong labor movement

00:00:24.601 --> 00:00:25.834
in a way we hadn't
for a long time?

00:00:25.834 --> 00:00:27.133
-You know, there's a few things.

00:00:27.133 --> 00:00:29.367
One, I think -- You know,
Alex Press is a really good

00:00:29.367 --> 00:00:31.267
labor reporter, who's been
writing for Jacobin.

00:00:31.267 --> 00:00:32.734
I had her on the podcast,
you know,

00:00:32.734 --> 00:00:34.400
talked about so much
of this stuff

00:00:34.400 --> 00:00:36.834
ends up coming about -- things
that are deeper than wages.

00:00:36.834 --> 00:00:39.067
It's about
autonomy and dignity.

00:00:39.067 --> 00:00:41.667
And I've talked to
labor organizers for many years.

00:00:41.667 --> 00:00:44.234
I used to be largely
a labor reporter.

00:00:44.234 --> 00:00:46.133
And I had an organizer
say something similar to me,

00:00:46.133 --> 00:00:48.667
that this stuff is always
deeper than wages.

00:00:48.667 --> 00:00:50.567
I mean, wages and healthcare
and benefits

00:00:50.567 --> 00:00:52.667
and work hours
and stuff like that matter,

00:00:52.667 --> 00:00:55.901
but when you're doing something
as risky as a strike,

00:00:55.901 --> 00:00:58.334
it's because some sense
of your dignity

00:00:58.334 --> 00:01:00.334
and your autonomy
as a human being

00:01:00.334 --> 00:01:02.200
has been
fundamentally insulted.

00:01:02.200 --> 00:01:05.434
And I think one of the things
is that we've seen COVID

00:01:05.434 --> 00:01:07.467
provoked
an enormous reassessment

00:01:07.467 --> 00:01:09.501
for a lot of people
about what your value

00:01:09.501 --> 00:01:10.901
and what your worth is,

00:01:10.901 --> 00:01:12.934
about who values you
and who doesn't value,

00:01:12.934 --> 00:01:14.601
whether the boss does or not,

00:01:14.601 --> 00:01:16.567
about what you want
to do with your life.

00:01:16.567 --> 00:01:19.667
And I think that, combined
with very tight labor markets,

00:01:19.667 --> 00:01:21.167
which I think have
sort of helped,

00:01:21.167 --> 00:01:24.300
and, also,
I think this real seeding

00:01:24.300 --> 00:01:26.767
of kind of left labor,
liberal ideas

00:01:26.767 --> 00:01:28.534
that were the product

00:01:28.534 --> 00:01:31.734
of the discrediting
of American global capitalism

00:01:31.734 --> 00:01:33.334
that happened
after the Great Recession.

00:01:33.334 --> 00:01:34.734
And we've seen that
grow and grow and grow.

00:01:34.734 --> 00:01:36.767
All that's kind of coming
together in this moment

00:01:36.767 --> 00:01:39.033
where you have more
strike action this year

00:01:39.033 --> 00:01:41.367
than we've literally seen
in decades.

00:01:41.367 --> 00:01:43.634
-I also think, you know,
the employers

00:01:43.634 --> 00:01:46.300
sometimes forget that
once, you know,

00:01:46.300 --> 00:01:49.067
these unions go on strike,
once these guilds go on strike,

00:01:49.067 --> 00:01:50.934
they've already shown
solidarity,

00:01:50.934 --> 00:01:54.868
and they underestimate
how much harder it is

00:01:54.868 --> 00:01:57.834
to get them back to work
without you making a concession.

00:01:57.834 --> 00:02:00.434
Because they don't want
to leave their jobs.

00:02:00.434 --> 00:02:01.901
They don't want
to be out of work.

00:02:01.901 --> 00:02:03.467
And when they make
that sacrifice

00:02:03.467 --> 00:02:06.701
and they put, you know,
their families

00:02:06.701 --> 00:02:08.033
at a disadvantage going out,

00:02:08.033 --> 00:02:09.567
you've got to then
bring something to the table.

00:02:09.567 --> 00:02:11.200
So don't let them go on strike.

00:02:11.200 --> 00:02:13.000
That's the first thing
you should try very hard to do.

00:02:13.000 --> 00:02:14.334
-I totally agree with that.

00:02:14.334 --> 00:02:16.501
And I think one of the things
you see is that

00:02:16.501 --> 00:02:19.634
because strikes had become
so rare in America

00:02:19.634 --> 00:02:21.634
over a long period of time,
there was disbelief.

00:02:21.634 --> 00:02:23.834
I mean, the WGA
is a little bit different

00:02:23.834 --> 00:02:25.334
because it has been
such a militant union.

00:02:25.334 --> 00:02:26.934
Obviously,
they had a strike in 2008.

00:02:26.934 --> 00:02:29.033
-Yep.
-But, again, that's 15 years.

00:02:29.033 --> 00:02:30.901
A lot has changed in 15 years.
-Yeah.

00:02:30.901 --> 00:02:33.267
-So, I think that
sort of a reminder of this --

00:02:33.267 --> 00:02:36.067
I mean, a strike
is a great tool for unions

00:02:36.067 --> 00:02:37.968
if you don't have
to use it, right?

00:02:37.968 --> 00:02:42.367
And so I do hope that these
strikes we're seeing this year

00:02:42.367 --> 00:02:44.467
have broader effects
throughout the economy

00:02:44.467 --> 00:02:46.534
in how folks in management
are thinking about that.

00:02:46.534 --> 00:02:48.100
-You have --
You mentioned your podcast.

00:02:48.100 --> 00:02:50.868
You have a wonderful podcast
and you're doing it live.

00:02:50.868 --> 00:02:53.100
Here we go. Yeah.
"Why Is This Happening?"

00:02:53.100 --> 00:02:54.767
And do you enjoy doing it
in front of a live audience?

00:02:54.767 --> 00:02:56.234
-Oh, I love it. I love it.

00:02:56.234 --> 00:02:57.634
I love --
I'm a theater kid at heart.

00:02:57.634 --> 00:03:00.367
I like --
-Yeah. I mean --

00:03:00.367 --> 00:03:02.234
-Yeah, you really are. I know.
[ Laughter ]

00:03:02.234 --> 00:03:04.767
I feel like people don't see
enough of that side of you.

00:03:04.767 --> 00:03:06.567
-Yeah, yeah.
-Yeah.

00:03:06.567 --> 00:03:08.067
I like that you were
a theater kid

00:03:08.067 --> 00:03:09.567
and your theater director
was like,

00:03:09.567 --> 00:03:11.634
"You should go out and talk
about political issues."

00:03:11.634 --> 00:03:13.234
-Yeah.

00:03:13.234 --> 00:03:14.667
-You're like, "I think I'd
make a good Hamlet."

00:03:14.667 --> 00:03:16.701
They're like,
"No talk about labor laws."

00:03:16.701 --> 00:03:19.033
[ Laughter ]

00:03:19.033 --> 00:03:21.067
-This is what I ended up doing.
It's so funny.

00:03:21.067 --> 00:03:23.667
-But, yeah, it's got
Chicago, Philadelphia, New York.

00:03:23.667 --> 00:03:24.868
It's a wonderful podcast.

00:03:24.868 --> 00:03:26.400
And you do, like,
really drill down on an issue

00:03:26.400 --> 00:03:27.968
in a way that's helpful.

00:03:27.968 --> 00:03:29.634
I love listening to it.
-Thank you very much.

00:03:29.634 --> 00:03:31.601
Yeah, we're really enjoying it.

00:03:31.601 --> 00:03:34.734
-You're not just a theater kid.
You're a real outdoorsman.

00:03:34.734 --> 00:03:37.234
And you posted
this photo recently,

00:03:37.234 --> 00:03:39.801
because a lot of people know
you from TV, and there you are.

00:03:39.801 --> 00:03:42.133
So, someone else cut down these
trees, and then you walked over?

00:03:42.133 --> 00:03:44.934
-That's exactly right.
That's exactly right.

00:03:44.934 --> 00:03:47.467
-Well, I know --
So, you did cut down the tree.

00:03:47.467 --> 00:03:49.801
-There was a lot of
hair and makeup just off set.

00:03:49.801 --> 00:03:50.834
-Right, yeah.

00:03:50.834 --> 00:03:52.434
-Right before
we set up the shot.

00:03:52.434 --> 00:03:55.133
-Yeah. They were like -- They're
misting you to make it look --

00:03:55.133 --> 00:03:57.033
-It was below the line workers
during the strike.

00:03:57.033 --> 00:03:58.634
I was like,
"I got a gig for you."

00:03:58.634 --> 00:04:01.868
-So, but you recently -- Who was
it that addressed the fact

00:04:01.868 --> 00:04:03.801
that you maybe weren't
what they expected to see --

00:04:03.801 --> 00:04:05.200
-So, my wife took this picture.

00:04:05.200 --> 00:04:06.934
We have a place upstate,
and I was chopping up

00:04:06.934 --> 00:04:08.467
some trees,
as you can see there.

00:04:08.467 --> 00:04:10.968
And I posted it
because I was in a bagel shop,

00:04:10.968 --> 00:04:12.400
and this guy
was serving me a bagel.

00:04:12.400 --> 00:04:14.467
And he sort of looks at me
and he goes,

00:04:14.467 --> 00:04:16.167
"Are you the guy from TV?"
I'm like, "Yeah."

00:04:16.167 --> 00:04:17.267
He's like, "You're Chris Hayes.
I'm like, "Yeah."

00:04:17.267 --> 00:04:19.200
He goes, "You know,
you look like

00:04:19.200 --> 00:04:22.000
a little accountant on TV,

00:04:22.000 --> 00:04:23.734
but in person,
you look like a ballplayer."

00:04:23.734 --> 00:04:24.767
-Wow!

00:04:24.767 --> 00:04:26.067
-I was like,
"Well, thank you, buddy."

00:04:26.067 --> 00:04:27.200
-That's really nice.
-I'm trying.

00:04:27.200 --> 00:04:28.667
-Yeah.

00:04:28.667 --> 00:04:32.267
-Just trying to remind everyone
I'm not a little accountant.

00:04:32.267 --> 00:04:34.067
That's why I had my wife
take a picture of me

00:04:34.067 --> 00:04:35.200
chopping down trees.

00:04:35.200 --> 00:04:36.968
-Cutting down trees,
going to get my bagel --

00:04:36.968 --> 00:04:38.000
tough-guy stuff.

00:04:38.000 --> 00:04:39.467
-Yeah, exactly.

00:04:39.467 --> 00:04:42.067
-I want to give you --
You're very issue-based,

00:04:42.067 --> 00:04:45.667
and I want to give you an issue
to make an argument for,

00:04:45.667 --> 00:04:47.667
because I know you feel
very passionate about this.

00:04:47.667 --> 00:04:50.367
As passionate as you feel
about labor laws

00:04:50.367 --> 00:04:51.634
and the plight
of the working man,

00:04:51.634 --> 00:04:54.868
you feel strongest that
minivans get a bad rap.

00:04:54.868 --> 00:04:58.534
-Minivans. Thank you!
Who -- Thank you!

00:04:58.534 --> 00:05:01.100
[ Cheers and applause ]
-I'm gonna give you 60 seconds.

00:05:01.100 --> 00:05:02.400
We're going to put it
on the board.

00:05:02.400 --> 00:05:05.400
Make a case, Chris Hayes,
for minivans. Go.

00:05:05.400 --> 00:05:06.868
-Alright, first negative case.

00:05:06.868 --> 00:05:09.467
The crossover is dead.
It looks terrible.

00:05:09.467 --> 00:05:12.767
Everyone's driving
some, like, knock-off S.U.V.

00:05:12.767 --> 00:05:14.133
Everyone -- What are you doing?

00:05:14.133 --> 00:05:16.567
You're dropping your kids off
in a Jeep?

00:05:16.567 --> 00:05:19.467
You're not going to war.
You're not driving on sand.

00:05:19.467 --> 00:05:21.100
You're not doing anything
you need a Jeep for.

00:05:21.100 --> 00:05:24.567
You're driving to third grade
to drop your kids off.

00:05:24.567 --> 00:05:26.801
You do not need
an enormous S.U.V.

00:05:26.801 --> 00:05:28.634
But if, like me,
you have three kids,

00:05:28.634 --> 00:05:33.601
if you're a family man,
a family woman, you need space.

00:05:33.601 --> 00:05:37.100
The van, the minivan,
in all its beautiful glory,

00:05:37.100 --> 00:05:41.133
is the sublime synthesis
of form following function.

00:05:41.133 --> 00:05:42.200
-Oh, my God.

00:05:42.200 --> 00:05:46.200
-When you drive
around in a minivan,

00:05:46.200 --> 00:05:48.267
you're saying to the world

00:05:48.267 --> 00:05:52.033
and using the correct tool
for the correct task,

00:05:52.033 --> 00:05:54.501
which is trucking
your kids around

00:05:54.501 --> 00:05:57.400
to endless things
they have to go do

00:05:57.400 --> 00:05:59.100
and not pretending
you're driving down a Jeep

00:05:59.100 --> 00:06:00.501
where you're going to war.

00:06:00.501 --> 00:06:02.701
-There you go. Chris Hayes.
[ Cheers and applause ]

00:06:02.701 --> 00:06:04.968
-So sad.

00:06:04.968 --> 00:06:09.934
It's so sad to see you in
the pocket of the minivan lobby.

00:06:09.934 --> 00:06:11.567
-That's all --
I'm not sponsored.

00:06:11.567 --> 00:06:13.133
I just want to be clear.
-Yeah, yeah, yeah.

00:06:13.133 --> 00:06:14.400
-As a newsman, there's no --

00:06:14.400 --> 00:06:16.367
-Yeah. I will say, I mean,
a minivan is great.

00:06:16.367 --> 00:06:19.067
When you -- When a friend
comes over with a minivan

00:06:19.067 --> 00:06:20.501
and the door opens
and the kids get out

00:06:20.501 --> 00:06:21.801
and you don't have to help them,
I'm like, "Why?"

00:06:21.801 --> 00:06:23.267
-You're not -- This move?
-This move sucks.

00:06:23.267 --> 00:06:24.434
-We don't need that move.

00:06:24.434 --> 00:06:25.834
-Yeah, I leave my kids
in the car now.

00:06:25.834 --> 00:06:27.534
-Yes.
[ Laughter ]

00:06:27.534 --> 00:06:29.567
-I drop them off at school
and then I'm like,

00:06:29.567 --> 00:06:30.767
"If you can't get out,
I'm taking you home."

00:06:30.767 --> 00:06:32.968
-"Get out."
-You guys, that's Chris Hayes.

00:06:32.968 --> 00:06:35.901
-"All In With Chris Hayes" airs
weeknights at 8:00 on MSNBC.

00:06:35.901 --> 00:06:36.968
Tickets are still available to

00:06:36.968 --> 00:06:38.300
the "Why Is This Happening?"
tour.

00:06:38.300 --> 00:06:39.634
We'll be right back with more
"Late Night."

